			<div class="content" id="post-content-2787959">
									

<p>Thank you!</p>
<p>I was really torn about whether to include the collapsible note from Director Jones or not&#8230; But in the feedback I got on an earlier draft someone pointed out that the personnel directly editing SCP containment procedures felt really wrong, and there must be a strict review system with the higher ups having the final say. The whole thing also seemed a bit too subtle without a very clear explanation that you're getting two opposing points of view mashed into a document that's meant to stay &quot;neutral&quot;. I wanted to tone down the impression that the very clusterfucky feeling was unintentional, you're meant to experience the conflict through the leftover subjective parts that managed to stay neutral and well-referenced enough to get approved. But in this version I flat out explain that there are revisions right at the beginning, so maybe it gets a bit redundant.</p>
<p>By tonal issues do you mean the bits of comic relief with the pettiness of the two head researchers/the most passive-aggressive footnotes? I can see how they might feel wrong and not really funny.</p>
<p>About development, I'm afraid that the Foundation having figured out too much about the message and the entities' intents would weaken the story&#8230; An important part of the story is the sense that the message is important but fading away and very difficult to figure out because it's from extremely advanced beings. Giving too much away would deter from the feeling that the item is still worth preserving and make the budget-conscious side clearly in the right, making the whole conflict pointless. But maybe you're right, there's probably room for a bit more information without crossing that line, just one piece of information still missing could be enough to warrant the conflict.</p>

							</div>

								
					
