			<div class="content" id="post-content-2794188">
									

<p>There's a lot going on, I'm not sure what exactly you don't get so I'll try to restate the main points a bit more clearly and with less technical jargon:</p>
<ul>
<li>The rock happens to contain a quartz crystal which, through random chance, crystallized in very homogeneous conditions. The result is that at a sub-atomic level, all the atoms are extremely similar. In this fiction, quarks (which are subatomic particles) have a chance of containing something similar to our universe at a very small scale (because within a quark, there can be tiny variations just like our galaxies and stars are just tiny pockets of energy in the void of space). I tried to make sure all that stuff is somewhat scientifically plausible, no &quot;magic&quot; involved!</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>Since an atom can contain a tiny universe, neighbouring atoms that are very similar can all individually generate a universe and the result will be very similar! So like parallel universes or alternate realities. This is what happened inside this rock.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>There is an extremely small chance of life being born, and then civilisation being born, and then this civilisation becoming extremely advanced, and then this civilisation trying and succeeding to send a message to an higher plane of existence in a given universe. But since there are trillions of trillions of atoms in the Earth, it was bound to happen eventually somewhere! This rock is amongst the ones in which it happened, and since there are a lot of &quot;copies&quot; of the universe, the message is detectable even though a message from a single atom would of course be undetectable.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>This civilisation has a lot figured out about reality, and they understand that beyond their two dimensions, and beyond OUR three dimensions, the plane of existence that is really special and really matters has 196,884 dimensions (this is supported by <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monster_group">actual math</a>! There is something about this amount of dimensions which is different and nobody understands exactly why, it just IS, embedded in the way numbers work. Spooky stuff.) Since they're a post-scarcity civilisation that has been freed long ago from animalistic desires and has explored and discovered everything, they want to contact those high-dimensional beings of course. But since they're only two-dimensional and trapped inside a tiny quark, they need US to carry their message across. Of course the only way would be to alter our universe in such a way that it would be visible to higher, presumably four-dimensional beings. So just destroy our universe to make it vibrate in the right pattern, exactly like they did to send us electromagnetic waves!</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The Foundation is starting to understand this. Of course this is a &quot;Safe&quot;, harmless item. But think of all the knowledge it contains! Unfortunately it is quite fragile since the tiniest disturbance has an effect on every atom and obviously the extremely small patterns that are their universes cannot withstand perturbations at our huge scale. Since the crystal is inside a thick layer of granite a few billions managed to survive anyway, but the signal is decreasing and the rock requires a lot of space and maintenance to ensure it decays as slowly as possible to keep recording the message.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The Foundation has a bunch of eldritch abominations to take care of though, and sinking a lot of money into maintaining this boring rock might seem a tad wasteful to some people. Thus two opposite sides arise: those who want to keep studying the rock and learn stuff from an extremely advanced civilisation that seems to have solved the fundamental nature of reality, and those who think the Foundation should use resources to stop Cthulhu instead of keeping a chunk of stone well-insulated for decades (not to mention that everything in the message seems aimed at getting us to destroy our universe to transmit the message to a higher realm).</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The controversy is so deep that both sides start using &quot;propaganda&quot; of sorts (while staying within the rules of the Foundation) to advance their agenda. Since technical documentation should stay neutral, but pretty much nobody amongst those who contribute to SCP documents is neutral on this issue, you're getting a bunch of edits from both points of view within the document (Supported by facts, as technical documentation should! But <strong>both</strong> sides are backed by undeniable facts.). I intended the reader to piece together the whole story through those glimpses at the internal conflict that pollute the factual, scientific core of the document.</li>
</ul>

